    "engine": "knitr",
    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Reports and exported tables (page 5)\"\n---\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n:::\n\n\nPage 5 of the app, **Report export**, gives you the results to keep: two reports (the box\n**Report**, left) and the tables of the data (the box **Export tabular data**, next to it). The\nreports use one normalization method; the tables can be exported for several.\n\n## Normalization method of the reports\n\nChoose the method first; the report buttons stay greyed out until you do. **Best** is the method with the\nlowest median RSD% among the QC replicates (here Multivariate (PCA)), and each option shows\nits median RSD%:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"datatables html-widget html-fill-item\" id=\"htmlwidget-599727308f7eaa2e0267\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-599727308f7eaa2e0267\">{\"x\":{\"filter\":\"none\",\"vertical\":false,\"data\":[[\"Best: Multivariate (PCA)\",\"Without normalization\",\"LOESS\",\"Closest RT\",\"Multivariate (PCA)\"],[26.5,31.5,27.8,28.6,26.5]],\"container\":\"<table class=\\\"display\\\">\\n  <thead>\\n    <tr>\\n      <th>Option<\\/th>\\n      <th>Median RSD%<\\/th>\\n    <\\/tr>\\n  <\\/thead>\\n<\\/table>\",\"options\":{\"dom\":\"t\",\"paging\":false,\"scrollX\":true,\"scrollY\":\"240px\",\"scrollCollapse\":true,\"columnDefs\":[{\"className\":\"dt-center\",\"targets\":\"_all\"},{\"name\":\"Option\",\"targets\":0},{\"name\":\"Median RSD%\",\"targets\":1}],\"order\":[],\"autoWidth\":false,\"orderClasses\":false}},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nChoose another method when, for example, 3.8 shows that it works clearly better for the compounds\nyou are most interested in.\n\n## Reports\n\n| Button | Report | For |\n|---|---|---|\n| **QC/QA report (html)** | the internal report: all quality-control steps, tables and interactive figures, as on pages 2–4 of the app | checking and documenting the processing |\n| **Summary report (pdf)** | the external report: the project, the methods and the results in a few pages | sharing the results |\n\nThe buttons are in the box **Report**, under the normalization method. A progress bar shows how far\nthe report is. When it is ready, its download button next to it (**Download HTML report**,\n**Download PDF report**) is no longer greyed out. The reports name the settings that were used, so\nthey document the processing.\n\nIn the QC/QA report, click a figure with a compound list (e.g. 4.5 or 4.6) to use the arrow keys\nthere: it gets a thin blue outline, and up / down go to the previous / next compound. Left / right\nswitch the modes where a figure has two (4.5, 4.7, and the view in 3.11), otherwise they also go\nthrough the compounds. Only the outlined figure reacts to the keys.\n\n## Exported tables\n\n**Export data into tables**, in the box **Export tabular data**, writes four Excel files for each\nticked normalization method (and each of their sheets as a `.csv` file), ready to download as one\n`.zip` file:\n\n| File | Content |\n|---|---|\n| `….norm.xlsx` | normalized peak areas of all features (also non-targeted) |\n| `….norm_blk.xlsx` | the same after blank subtraction (never with the blanks) |\n| `….conc.xlsx` | concentrations of the targeted compounds |\n| `….history.xlsx` | what the processing did with each feature and sample (see [Processing history](#processing-history)) |\n\nThe options of the box choose the columns of the tables:\n\n- **Normalization methods**: one set of files per ticked method (the best one is ticked at first).\n- **Group per injection type** (ticked at first): the QCs together, the blanks together, the\n  calibration points together and each study sample group together. Drag the groups (shown as\n  columns) into the order you want: the leftmost is the first group of the table. At first: QCs,\n  the study sample groups, blanks, calibration points.\n- **Group per batch**: the batches one after the other, by injection order within each batch (with\n  the grouping above too: by type within each batch).\n- **Keep in the tables**: the QCs, blanks and calibration points (all ticked at first). The study\n  samples are always in the tables.\n- **File formats**: Excel (`.xlsx`), CSV (one `.csv` file per sheet, without the *Legend* sheet),\n  or both (both ticked at first).\n\nWith outlier removal off (processing settings), the outlier injections found by the internal\nstandards (3.3) stay in the data: in the tables, their names are in red and the row *IS outlier\n(not removed)* gives the number of outlying internal standards. The history file has a sheet\n*Settings* with the processing settings of the results.\n\n**Download parameters (.yml)** under the export saves these options with the processing settings, in\nthe same file as on page 1; loading the file sets them again.\n\nOn SciLifeLab Serve, very large tables (more than 55 million values, e.g. 60,000 features in\n900 injections) are written as `.csv` files only, with the same layout, since an Excel file\nof that size takes too much memory to create there. This concerns the table of peak areas, and\nthe export box says so in advance. Run locally, QuaNTA always writes the Excel files as well.\n\nThis is the start of a batch sheet of the concentration table of the example\n(QuaNTA_example_ESIpos.mvis.conc.xlsx), the feature columns first and then the samples:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"datatables html-widget html-fill-item\" id=\"htmlwidget-a128edb91fb94de44437\" style=\"width:100%;height:auto;\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-a128edb91fb94de44437\">{\"x\":{\"filter\":\"none\",\"vertical\":false,\"data\":[[\"(+)-Nootkatone\",\"1-Methyl-1H-benzotriazole\",\"1,3-benzothiazole\",\"1,3-Di-o-tolylguanidine\",\"1,3-Diphenylguanidine\",\"1H-Benzotriazole\",\"3'-Hydroxycotinine\",\"Acetylcarnitine\",\"Aminocaproic acid\",\"Arnicolide C\",\"Asymmetr